WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.599
A program fills 512 megabytes of memory.

2
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:12.484
Then it calls fork, the system call that starts a second process as an exact copy of the first.

3
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:20.156
Copying 512 megabytes takes 33 milliseconds here, and needs another 512 megabytes of RAM.

4
00:00:20.156 --> 00:00:24.551
Yet the copy is running 1.7 milliseconds after fork.

5
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.544
How do you copy something faster than copying it?

6
00:00:28.033 --> 00:00:29.251
A quick reminder.

7
00:00:29.251 --> 00:00:31.900
Memory comes in pages of 4 kilobytes.

8
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:40.064
Each process has a page table: the kernel's list of which frame, which piece of real RAM, holds each of its pages.

9
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.305
Each line also says whether that page may be written.

10
00:00:44.305 --> 00:00:46.784
Here's a parent with four pages.

11
00:00:46.784 --> 00:00:49.031
The real program has 131,072.

12
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:51.275
Now let's design fork ourselves.

13
00:00:51.275 --> 00:00:55.031
The simplest version copies every page into fresh frames.

14
00:00:55.031 --> 00:00:58.061
It's correct, and slow exactly where it hurts.

15
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:04.300
Redis, a program that keeps data in memory, forks a child just to write a snapshot to disk.

16
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.767
With 10 gigabytes of data, a child that only reads would need 20 gigabytes of RAM.

17
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:15.349
Or a program forks only to start another program, which throws the copy away at once.

18
00:01:15.349 --> 00:01:20.732
That's photocopying a whole cookbook to read one recipe, then throwing it in the bin.

19
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.270
Next try: share.

20
00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:27.170
The child gets its own page table, pointing at the parent's frames.

21
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:32.363
That costs only the table, about 1 megabyte for 512 megabytes of pages.

22
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.783
But now, if the parent writes to page 2, the child sees the change.

23
00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:39.340
And the child was promised its own copy.

24
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.987
A café kitchen has the same problem.

25
00:01:41.987 --> 00:01:46.626
A second cook joins, and is promised their own copy of the recipe binder.

26
00:01:46.626 --> 00:01:51.645
Photocopying all of it would take all morning, and they'll mostly just read it.

27
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:54.119
So the manager does something lazier.

28
00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:59.121
Both cooks share the binder, and every page gets a sticker: ask before you write.

29
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:07.636
When a cook wants to change page 2, the manager photocopies just page 2, and that copy becomes their page 2, with no sticker.

30
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.690
Let's map it.

31
00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.880
The cooks are the two processes.

32
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.482
The sheets of paper are frames of RAM.

33
00:02:13.482 --> 00:02:18.068
The sticker is a read-only mark, on every line of both page tables.

34
00:02:18.068 --> 00:02:20.121
And the manager is the kernel.

35
00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.592
So here's what fork really does.

36
00:02:22.592 --> 00:02:29.633
It builds the child's table pointing at the same frames, and marks every line read-only, the parent's too.

37
00:02:29.633 --> 00:02:32.555
Each frame now has two lines pointing at it.

38
00:02:32.555 --> 00:02:33.485
No data moved.

39
00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:35.245
The child reads page 1.

40
00:02:35.245 --> 00:02:42.180
Reading is allowed, so the CPU loads it straight from frame B, and the kernel never hears about it.

41
00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:44.451
Now the child writes to page 2.

42
00:02:44.451 --> 00:02:50.258
The line says read-only, so the CPU stops the write, and hands control to the kernel.

43
00:02:50.258 --> 00:02:52.103
That's called a page fault.

44
00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:57.349
The kernel sees that the page may really be written, and that frame C is shared.

45
00:02:57.349 --> 00:03:00.929
So it copies frame C's 4 kilobytes into a free frame, E.

46
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.495
It points the child's line at E, and makes it writable.

47
00:03:04.495 --> 00:03:07.087
Then the write runs again, and lands in E.

48
00:03:07.233 --> 00:03:09.373
Now the parent writes to page 2.

49
00:03:09.373 --> 00:03:12.448
Its line is still read-only, so it faults too.

50
00:03:12.448 --> 00:03:14.788
Will the kernel copy frame C again?

51
00:03:14.788 --> 00:03:15.857
Pause and guess.

52
00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:19.239
No.

53
00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:23.147
Only one line points at C now, so nobody else can see it.

54
00:03:23.147 --> 00:03:27.672
The kernel just makes the line writable again, and copies nothing.

55
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:33.604
This is called copy-on-write: share every page, and copy one only when someone writes to it.

56
00:03:33.604 --> 00:03:39.369
The kernel counts the lines pointing at each frame, so it knows when to copy and when not to.

57
00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:43.538
In the lab, the kernel reports each process's memory.

58
00:03:43.538 --> 00:03:49.871
Right after fork, the child shows 512 megabytes shared with the parent, and 0 of its own.

59
00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:54.546
Then it writes one byte in every fourth page.

60
00:03:54.546 --> 00:04:02.368
The kernel makes 32,768 copies, 128 megabytes, at about 1.8 microseconds each.

61
00:04:02.368 --> 00:04:07.483
Now the child shows 384 shared, and 128 of its own.

62
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:08.356
One trap.

63
00:04:08.356 --> 00:04:14.150
RSS, the usual memory number, counts every page a process has in RAM.

64
00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:18.769
It says 513 for each, so adding them claims a gigabyte.

65
00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:20.448
They really use 640.

66
00:04:20.633 --> 00:04:29.012
PSS splits each shared page between its sharers, and the two PSS numbers add up to exactly 640.

67
00:04:29.012 --> 00:04:31.569
So to add up memory, add PSS.

68
00:04:31.933 --> 00:04:34.255
Copy-on-write isn't free, though.

69
00:04:34.255 --> 00:04:41.077
When the child wrote to every page, the faults took 180 milliseconds, several times one big copy.

70
00:04:41.233 --> 00:04:48.110
The kitchen agrees: asking the manager 131,072 times takes longer than one trip to the copy shop.

71
00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:55.199
Copy-on-write wins because most children write a little, or start another program and write nothing.

72
00:04:55.567 --> 00:04:59.494
So the moral of the story: fork copies the list, not the memory.

73
00:04:59.494 --> 00:05:04.220
Everything is shared until someone writes, and then only that page is copied.

74
00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:05.534
One quick check.

75
00:05:05.534 --> 00:05:09.326
A child only reads, like Redis writing its snapshot.

76
00:05:09.326 --> 00:05:11.952
How many pages does the kernel copy?

77
00:05:11.952 --> 00:05:13.119
Pause and think.

78
00:05:16.367 --> 00:05:16.722
None.

79
00:05:16.722 --> 00:05:22.545
Reads never fault, so the child can run for minutes without costing a single copy.
